Cancel retransmission of every reliable packet an ACK covers; keep a NACK exact.

Steam acknowledges cumulatively: an ACK for packet n means it has every reliable packet up to
n. The retransmission queue cancelled only the packet whose id the ACK named, so when a
packet's own ACK was lost its timer kept resending it until it ran out of attempts, even though
a later ACK had already told us it arrived. During play, with the control channel carrying
250 reliable HID reports a second, that wasted a steady slice of the uplink.

IHS_RetransmissionCancel now takes a `cumulative` flag. The receive path sets it for an ACK
and clears it for a NACK: an ACK marks as acknowledged every pending packet on the channel at
or before the acked id, by 16-bit wraparound (IHS_RetransmissionSeqCovered), while a NACK -
which names one packet the host is still missing - marks only that exact id, as before. A
cumulative NACK would cancel packets the host never received and silently drop HID input.

The channel's newest cumulatively-acked id is remembered (ackedId/ackedValid, a NACK never
advances it) so a packet a later ACK already covered can be dropped instead of resent forever
(used by 0026). The round trip is still sampled only from the exact packet the ACK names, since
an earlier packet it also covers may have been sent more than once. Marking under the lock keeps
patch 0019's ownership model: the timer task still frees the item. The wraparound compare is
correct only while no two live pending ids are more than 32767 apart, which the control backlog
cap (0026) keeps true.

